-- title:
--   A finite measure on a path space is determined by its finite-dimensional marginals
-- statement:
--   Let $(X_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ be a family of measurable spaces and let $\mu,\nu$ be finite measures on the path space $\prod_{n} X_n$. If for every $n$ the two measures have the same image under the restriction map
--   $$\mathrm{fr}_n : \prod_{k} X_k \to \prod_{k \le n} X_k, \qquad \omega \mapsto (\omega_k)_{k \le n},$$
--   then $\mu = \nu$.
--
--   **Why it is needed.** The product $\sigma$-algebra on a path space is generated by cylinder sets, so a measure on $\prod_n X_n$ is determined by its finite-dimensional marginals. This is used constantly and informally — "two processes with the same finite-dimensional distributions have the same law" — but in a formalization it is not a one-liner: the marginals live in *different* types as $n$ varies, and the argument has to route through the projective-limit machinery.
--
--   Concretely, any statement about a process built by the Ionescu–Tulcea theorem (`Kernel.traj`) that is not already in the library's API has to be proved by comparing finite-dimensional marginals, and this lemma is what licenses that reduction. Typical uses: identifying the law of a shifted trajectory, proving stationarity of a chain started from an invariant measure, or showing that two different constructions of the same process agree.
--
--   **Why finiteness matters.** Agreement of all finite-dimensional marginals does *not* determine a general (possibly non-$\sigma$-finite) measure on a path space, so some finiteness hypothesis is genuinely needed; `IsFiniteMeasure` on both sides is the form in which it is always available in probability, where the measures are probability measures.
--
--   **Restriction to intervals $\mathrm{Iic}\,n$ suffices.** Only the *initial segments* $\{k : k \le n\}$ are tested, not all finite subsets. This is a real simplification for the user — checking one marginal per natural number rather than one per finite subset — and is legitimate because every finite subset of $\mathbb{N}$ is contained in some initial segment, so the initial-segment marginals already determine the whole projective family.
--
--   **Proof.** The family $n \mapsto \mu \circ \mathrm{fr}_n^{-1}$ is projective: restricting the $b$-th marginal to coordinates $\le a$ gives the $a$-th, because the restriction maps compose. Hence the induced family indexed by finite subsets is a projective measure family, and $\mu$ is its projective limit — by the criterion that it suffices to check the defining property on intervals $\mathrm{Iic}\,n$. By hypothesis $\nu$ is the projective limit of the *same* family. Uniqueness of projective limits of finite measures gives $\mu = \nu$.
-- source:
--   A. N. Kolmogorov, Grundbegriffe der Wahrscheinlichkeitsrechnung, Springer 1933, §III (consistency/extension theorem); J. Neveu, Mathematical Foundations of the Calculus of Probability, Holden-Day 1965, Ch. III; O. Kallenberg, Foundations of Modern Probability, 2nd ed., Springer 2002, Theorem 6.16.

import Mathlib.Probability.Kernel.IonescuTulcea.Traj

open Filter Finset Function MeasurableEquiv MeasurableSpace MeasureTheory Preorder ProbabilityTheory
open scoped ENNReal NNReal Topology

theorem PathSpace.ext_of_map_frestrictLe {X : ℕ → Type*} [∀ n, MeasurableSpace (X n)]
    {μ ν : Measure (Π n, X n)} [IsFiniteMeasure μ] [IsFiniteMeasure ν]
    (h : ∀ n, μ.map (frestrictLe n) = ν.map (frestrictLe n)) : μ = ν := by sorry
